{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01S2R7FgsQw6Ac9o5c2gJQBR","type":"tool_result","content":"1:# Chapter 8: Standing the Watch\n2:## *\"The commands you'll actually type, and the versions that bite\"*\n8:<!-- AUTHOR-REVIEW: the KCNA curriculum snapshot is not in this chapter's cached source set (the corpus note lists only the 18 snapshots referenced by this chapter's pipeline-state files). The house metadata form used by Chapters 2, 5 and 7 carries two inline CNCF source tags on this line. Restore them from the book-level curriculum snapshot during revision rather than citing blind. -->\n12:## Attention Budget\n47:## 🧭 Soundings\n96:## Why This Chapter Matters\n112:## What You'll Learn\n127:## ⚪ §1 — The Grammar of a Command\n142:<!-- FIGURE: ch08-fig01-kubectl-verb-resource-grammar -->\n164:### The verb surface\n190:### Where the cluster came from\n198:### The surprising case: `kubectl` inside a Pod\n206:### The command that opens the chapter\n216:## 🔵 §2 — Three Gates and a Logbook\n224:[Omitted long matching line]\n226:### Gate one: authentication — *who are you?*\n232:### Gate two: authorization — *may you do this?*\n238:### Gate three: admission control — *should this, exactly as written, be allowed?*\n248:<!-- FIGURE: ch08-fig02-three-api-gates -->\n271:### Two admission controllers you have already met\n281:<!-- AUTHOR-REVIEW: no cached source states that ResourceQuota and LimitRange are enforced by admission controllers. The claim is standard and near-certain, but it is unsourced here. The controlling-access/ fetch flagged above, or the admission-controllers reference page, should close it. -->\n285:### And a logbook\n291:<!-- AUTHOR-REVIEW: deliberately minimal. No cached source defines what an audit record contains, what stages are recorded, or that auditing is policy-driven. Per the outline's Open Question #4 the recommendation was option (b) — name it, assert no definition. If Stage 2 fetches kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/debug/debug-cluster/audit/ (same doc tree as the two blocking fetches), upgrade this to two or three sentences during revision. -->\n293:### Why three gates at one door is a complete story\n303:## 🔵 §3 — Dividing a Shared Cluster\n313:[Omitted long matching line]\n315:### The discrimination, made structural\n327:<!-- FIGURE: ch08-fig05-quota-vs-limitrange -->\n352:### The hinge, and it is worth thirty seconds\n366:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings #1 — The Path a Command Takes In\n418:## 🔵 §4 — Taking a Node Out of Service\n422:### Where nodes come from\n430:### The three commands\n438:<!-- FIGURE: ch08-fig04-node-lifecycle-cordon-drain -->\n461:### Node conditions\n483:### Heartbeats, and a control loop you already know\n493:### Capacity and Allocatable\n499:[Omitted long matching line]\n505:## ⚪ §5 — Who Owns the Control Plane\n511:### The planning questions\n523:### The tools, split by what they are for\n531:### One requirement none of these removes\n539:### What ownership actually means\n555:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings #2 — The Machine, and Whose It Is\n610:## 🟡 §6 — Versions That Are Allowed to Disagree\n620:### The vocabulary\n626:### The cadence, which makes the branch count make sense\n636:### The rules\n650:<!-- FIGURE: ch08-fig03-version-skew-window -->\n668:### The exception, which is where the exam points are\n682:### What the rule implies about upgrade order\n694:## 🔵 §7 — The One Backup That Matters\n706:### The mechanics\n712:<!-- AUTHOR-REVIEW: the TLS flags are named above because the backup snapshot lists them. No configuration guidance is given, deliberately: the etcd-access-control snapshot's note states that the source page's TLS configuration guidance was not verbatim-verified in that fetch. Do not expand this into how to configure etcd TLS without a fresh verified fetch. -->\n716:### The fact that matters more than the commands\n738:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings #3 — The Two Things You Cannot Improvise\n800:## ⚪ §8 — Rules, or Consequences\n824:<!-- FIGURE