{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XwfGN28rtacf9jaMb76NZy","type":"tool_result","content":"309:**ResourceQuota.** Namespaces are a way to divide cluster resources between multiple users, via resource quota [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23]. That is the sentence Chapter 4 gave you and then deferred *[cross-bearing: see Ch 4 §6 — namespaces, and what they are for]*. A quota is a ceiling on a **namespace, in aggregate**: the team's total, not any one Pod's numbers.\n321:**What happens to a manifest that says nothing about resources?** The quota may refuse it. The LimitRange may fill it in.\n325:> ★ **Fixed Point:** **ResourceQuota counts the namespace. LimitRange constrains the object.** One is a ceiling on a team; the other is a rule about a manifest.\n348:> ⚓ **Worth Securing:** The two are usually deployed together, for a reason worth understanding rather than memorising. A quota with no LimitRange means a single team member who omits resource requests can, with one Pod, consume the namespace's entire allocation, because nothing forced them to declare what they intended to use. The quota sets the ceiling; the LimitRange makes sure everyone's consumption is legible to it.\n354:ResourceQuota and LimitRange are namespaced objects. The Nodes that §4 is about to discuss are not.\n362:And one closing observation, offered rather than promised: both of these mechanisms take effect at the admission gate. Neither is a separate subsystem with its own enforcement path. That is the first instalment of a claim §8 will finish.\n497:[Omitted long matching line]\n499:[Omitted long matching line]\n541:Also collect one small debt here: scheduler profile configuration lives in the control plane's own component configuration, which is a thing you can reach only if you own the control plane *[cross-bearing: see Ch 7 §6 — scheduling profiles]*.\n543:That is the general shape of the answer to Soundings question 8. A managed control plane means the provider decides when you upgrade, and the provider holds the etcd backup. A self-hosted control plane means both are yours."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d2288c80-51a4-4d10-a21d-291edfb119a7","uuid":"662437a9-f2ca-4fe7-98bf-448fac334a30","timestamp":"2026-08-24T23:31:41.453Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"309:**ResourceQuota.** Namespaces are a way to divide cluster resources between multiple users, via resource quota [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23]. That is the sentence Chapter 4 gave you and then deferred *[cross-bearing: see Ch 4 §6 — namespaces, and what they are for]*. A quota is a ceiling on a **namespace, in aggregate**: the team's total, not any one Pod's numbers.\n321:**What happens to a manifest that says nothing about resources?** The quota may refuse it. The LimitRange may fill it in.\n325:> ★ **Fixed Point:** **ResourceQuota counts the namespace. LimitRange constrains the object.** One is a ceiling on a team; the other is a rule about a manifest.\n348:> ⚓ **Worth Securing:** The two are usually deployed together, for a reason worth understanding rather than memorising. A quota with no LimitRange means a single team member who omits resource requests can, with one Pod, consume the namespace's entire allocation, because nothing forced them to declare what they intended to use. The quota sets the ceiling; the LimitRange makes sure everyone's consumption is legible to it.\n354:ResourceQuota and LimitRange are namespaced objects. The Nodes that §4 is about to discuss are not.\n362:And one closing observation, offered rather than promised: both of these mechanisms take effect at the admission gate. Neither is a separate subsystem with its own enforcement path. That is the first instalment of a claim §8 will finish.\n497:[Omitted long matching line]\n499:[Omitted long matching line]\n541:Also collect one small debt here: scheduler profile configuration lives in the control plane's own component configuration, which is a thing you can reach only if you own the control plane *[cross-bearing: see Ch 7 §6 — scheduling profiles]*.\n543:That is the general shape of the answer to Soundings question 8. A managed control plane means the provider decides when you upgrade, and the provider holds the etcd backup. A self-hosted control plane means both are yours.","numLines":10,"totalLines":10}}
