{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018HBxebBCZRo7FbiMuQVmD2","type":"tool_result","content":"493\t### Capacity and Allocatable\n494\t\n495\tChapter 7 §2 told you that what makes Capacity and Allocatable differ, and how that is configured, is this chapter's material *[cross-bearing: see Ch 7 §2 — Capacity, Allocatable, and what the scheduler counts]*. Here is the honest, partial payment.\n496\t\n497\t'Allocatable' on a Kubernetes node is defined as the amount of compute resources that are available for pods [source: k8s-docs-node-allocatable-2026-08-24]. The scheduler treats 'Allocatable' as the available capacity for Pods, and does not over-subscribe it [source: k8s-docs-node-allocatable-2026-08-24]. Capacity is the machine's total; Allocatable is what is left for your workloads after the node's own overheads are set aside. The two differ because some of the machine has to be reserved for the software that makes it a node at all: the kubelet, the container runtime, and the operating system underneath both.\n498\t\n499\t<!-- AUTHOR-REVIEW: the sentence above beginning \"The two differ because\" is NOT sourced. The node-allocatable snapshot's extraction note is explicit that the Capacity → Allocatable relationship appears only as an image (node-capacity.svg) with no text equivalent, and that kube-reserved, system-reserved, eviction-threshold and cgroup enforcement were deliberately not extracted. Per outline Open Question #6 the recommendation was option (b) — pay the Chapter 7 promise partially by naming what the reservation is FOR without the arithmetic. That is what the sentence does, but it needs either a source or removal. Fetch kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/reserve-compute-resources/ if the promise is to be paid properly; otherwise cut to the two verbatim-sourced sentences and note in Ch 7's revision that the pointer is chapter-scoped rather than section-pinned. Do NOT state an arithmetic relationship in any case. -->\n500\t\n501\tThe configuration detail — how much is reserved and by which flag — is above the associate tier and this book does not cover it. What the exam wants from you is the distinction: **Allocatable is the number the scheduler does arithmetic against.**\n502\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d2288c80-51a4-4d10-a21d-291edfb119a7","uuid":"983fa2ae-799f-41f2-8cb0-cf8636441987","timestamp":"2026-08-24T23:31:53.246Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/.pipeline-state/ch-08/draft-v1.md","content":"### Capacity and Allocatable\n\nChapter 7 §2 told you that what makes Capacity and Allocatable differ, and how that is configured, is this chapter's material *[cross-bearing: see Ch 7 §2 — Capacity, Allocatable, and what the scheduler counts]*. Here is the honest, partial payment.\n\n'Allocatable' on a Kubernetes node is defined as the amount of compute resources that are available for pods [source: k8s-docs-node-allocatable-2026-08-24]. The scheduler treats 'Allocatable' as the available capacity for Pods, and does not over-subscribe it [source: k8s-docs-node-allocatable-2026-08-24]. Capacity is the machine's total; Allocatable is what is left for your workloads after the node's own overheads are set aside. The two differ because some of the machine has to be reserved for the software that makes it a node at all: the kubelet, the container runtime, and the operating system underneath both.\n\n<!-- AUTHOR-REVIEW: the sentence above beginning \"The two differ because\" is NOT sourced. The node-allocatable snapshot's extraction note is explicit that the Capacity → Allocatable relationship appears only as an image (node-capacity.svg) with no text equivalent, and that kube-reserved, system-reserved, eviction-threshold and cgroup enforcement were deliberately not extracted. Per outline Open Question #6 the recommendation was option (b) — pay the Chapter 7 promise partially by naming what the reservation is FOR without the arithmetic. That is what the sentence does, but it needs either a source or removal. Fetch kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/reserve-compute-resources/ if the promise is to be paid properly; otherwise cut to the two verbatim-sourced sentences and note in Ch 7's revision that the pointer is chapter-scoped rather than section-pinned. Do NOT state an arithmetic relationship in any case. -->\n\nThe configuration detail — how much is reserved and by which flag — is above the associate tier and this book does not cover it. What the exam wants from you is the distinction: **Allocatable is the number the scheduler does arithmetic against.**\n","numLines":10,"startLine":493,"totalLines":1132}}}
