{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01G7HXi1PauNsPiDdrNfUsSn","type":"tool_result","content":"812\t**§3.** A ResourceQuota is an object. You `apply` it exactly as you apply a Deployment. It takes effect because something reads it at the gate when other requests arrive, which means a quota is a **declaration that changes what other declarations are permitted to say.** Nothing about it is a special-case enforcement engine.\n813\t\n814\t**§4, and this is the one that should land.** `kubectl cordon` has no private channel to the node. It does not connect to the machine. It writes a field on a Node object through the API server, and Chapter 7's built-in taint machinery — the `node.kubernetes.io/unschedulable` taint you met in a table with a `NoSchedule` effect — was already watching for exactly that. The scheduler then does what the scheduler always does: it checks taints when it makes scheduling decisions [source: k8s-docs-taints-tolerations-depth-2026-08-24], finds one it did not find before, and places nothing there. Nothing about `cordon` is new. It is Chapter 7's mechanism with an operator's hand on it.\n815\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d2288c80-51a4-4d10-a21d-291edfb119a7","uuid":"a0d43219-268e-4e68-acc7-9d42bd6071f1","timestamp":"2026-08-24T23:31:58.188Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/.pipeline-state/ch-08/draft-v1.md","content":"**§3.** A ResourceQuota is an object. You `apply` it exactly as you apply a Deployment. It takes effect because something reads it at the gate when other requests arrive, which means a quota is a **declaration that changes what other declarations are permitted to say.** Nothing about it is a special-case enforcement engine.\n\n**§4, and this is the one that should land.** `kubectl cordon` has no private channel to the node. It does not connect to the machine. It writes a field on a Node object through the API server, and Chapter 7's built-in taint machinery — the `node.kubernetes.io/unschedulable` taint you met in a table with a `NoSchedule` effect — was already watching for exactly that. The scheduler then does what the scheduler always does: it checks taints when it makes scheduling decisions [source: k8s-docs-taints-tolerations-depth-2026-08-24], finds one it did not find before, and places nothing there. Nothing about `cordon` is new. It is Chapter 7's mechanism with an operator's hand on it.\n","numLines":4,"startLine":812,"totalLines":1132}}}
