{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EorRTbh14ZmJhpgWZJqY2E","type":"tool_result","content":"311\t**LimitRange.** The Kubernetes security guidance states the pair's purposes in a single sentence: define ResourceQuotas to fairly allocate shared resources, and use LimitRanges to ensure that Pods specify their resource requirements [source: k8s-docs-cloud-native-security-2026-08-23]. Read that carefully, because the two halves are doing different jobs. A quota *allocates*. A LimitRange *ensures Pods specify*, which is a constraint on **individual objects**, and a mechanism that has to be able to act on a manifest that says nothing at all.\n312\t\n313\t<!-- AUTHOR-REVIEW: BLOCKING GAP. The cached sources support exactly two claims — that quota is the mechanism by which namespaces divide cluster resources among users (namespaces snapshot), and the functional contrast in the one cloud-native-security sentence quoted above. NOT cached and therefore NOT asserted below: what a ResourceQuota can count (compute totals, object counts, storage); the rule that once a quota exists in a namespace, Pods must specify requests and limits; LimitRange's min/max/default structure. This section is deliberately shorter than the two published cross-bearings pointing at it deserve. Fetch kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/policy/resource-quotas/ and .../limit-ranges/. Scope guard: take quota scope and what it counts, the requests-must-be-specified rule, and LimitRange's default/min/max shape. Do NOT take quota scope-selectors, priority-class quota, or the full countable-resource list — all above associate tier. -->\n314\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d2288c80-51a4-4d10-a21d-291edfb119a7","uuid":"a78ecf66-3df8-485f-bebb-e3a106f37ff5","timestamp":"2026-08-24T23:31:59.965Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/.pipeline-state/ch-08/draft-v1.md","content":"**LimitRange.** The Kubernetes security guidance states the pair's purposes in a single sentence: define ResourceQuotas to fairly allocate shared resources, and use LimitRanges to ensure that Pods specify their resource requirements [source: k8s-docs-cloud-native-security-2026-08-23]. Read that carefully, because the two halves are doing different jobs. A quota *allocates*. A LimitRange *ensures Pods specify*, which is a constraint on **individual objects**, and a mechanism that has to be able to act on a manifest that says nothing at all.\n\n<!-- AUTHOR-REVIEW: BLOCKING GAP. The cached sources support exactly two claims — that quota is the mechanism by which namespaces divide cluster resources among users (namespaces snapshot), and the functional contrast in the one cloud-native-security sentence quoted above. NOT cached and therefore NOT asserted below: what a ResourceQuota can count (compute totals, object counts, storage); the rule that once a quota exists in a namespace, Pods must specify requests and limits; LimitRange's min/max/default structure. This section is deliberately shorter than the two published cross-bearings pointing at it deserve. Fetch kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/policy/resource-quotas/ and .../limit-ranges/. Scope guard: take quota scope and what it counts, the requests-must-be-specified rule, and LimitRange's default/min/max shape. Do NOT take quota scope-selectors, priority-class quota, or the full countable-resource list — all above associate tier. -->\n","numLines":4,"startLine":311,"totalLines":1132}}}
